WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.882
France is a country where artists, intellectuals, and philosophers find inspiration.

2
00:00:05.883 --> 00:00:10.216
For romantics, it is a place where hopes and dreams might come true.

3
00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:15.939
Many people fall under France’s enchanting spell, and Amy March was one of them.

4
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:21.017
Amy found Fred to be everything she had ever hoped for in a suitor:

5
00:00:21.018 --> 00:00:28.052
he was from a very wealthy family, and he was handsome, intelligent, charming, and well groomed.

6
00:00:28.053 --> 00:00:33.629
There was no question that he adored Amy, practically worshipping the ground she walked on.

7
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:40.178
They went to parties together, walked in the gardens, visited museums, and attended the theater several times.

8
00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:47.798
While she was enjoying these activities and Fred’s company, Amy tried to convince herself that he was a perfect match for marriage

9
00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:53.152
(that is, if her parents allowed her to marry at 18, which would be in one year).

10
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.301
But something kept tugging at Amy’s heart.

11
00:00:56.302 --> 00:01:04.870
"Fred is perfect," Amy said to her reflection in the mirror as she got ready for a party that evening. "So why do I feel this way?"

12
00:01:04.871 --> 00:01:10.682
Until then, Amy had regarded marriage as a way to secure her position in society.

13
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:14.512
She did not really believe that love had to be involved.

14
00:01:14.513 --> 00:01:19.950
It was more important to her that she marry the right person rather than a poor artist.

15
00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:26.647
"Love will come later as I grow fond of my husband and respect him," she told herself over and over again.

16
00:01:26.648 --> 00:01:34.072
However, after meeting Fred, Amy could not put into practice what she thought she had strongly believed all these years.

17
00:01:34.073 --> 00:01:38.948
"Could I have been wrong?" she asked the confused image facing her in the mirror.

18
00:01:38.949 --> 00:01:46.624
For Jo, love was the last thing on her mind as she busied herself in her duties as a governess to the Kirke girls.

19
00:01:46.625 --> 00:01:51.677
Jo taught them how to sew, bake bread, whistle, play soccer, and love reading.

20
00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:59.259
Her days were so busy with the girls that she had no time to think about the problems at home, and she didn’t mind that.

21
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:09.194
Evenings were trickier since Jo’s responsibilities ended officially at four o’clock, and she had the rest of the day and evening to do as she pleased.

22
00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:15.712
In order not to fret about Beth, about Laurie, about Amy off in Europe, and about Meg coping with married life,

23
00:02:15.713 --> 00:02:23.028
Jo would either work on her writing or shut herself in the library downstairs and escape into a world of fiction.

24
00:02:23.029 --> 00:02:31.486
On one such evening Jo had gotten settled in the library and was about to begin reading chapter four of Jacob’s Mysterious Guest

25
00:02:31.487 --> 00:02:34.904
, when she heard a man come through the front

26
00:02:34.905 --> 00:02:43.009
entrance. She buried her face in the book, praying that whoever it was would take no notice of her, since she was not particularly in a sociable

27
00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:49.385
mood. As luck would have it, the man passed by the open library door without stopping to see who was

28
00:02:49.386 --> 00:02:58.137
inside. Jo, pulling the book away from her face let out a sigh of relief, but that quickly changed into a

29
00:02:58.138 --> 00:03:02.994
scream. For there was the man she thought had already gone, standing in the

30
00:03:02.995 --> 00:03:08.361
doorway. "Forgive me, miss," the man apologized profusely. "I didn’t mean to frighten

31
00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:14.785
you." Still shaking from the shock, Jo was unable to speak, but her eyes fell upon his stocking

32
00:03:14.786 --> 00:03:19.763
feet, more specifically upon his left sock, which had a rather large hole in

33
00:03:19.764 --> 00:03:26.668
it. "You see, I thought you were one of the girls trying to hide so she wouldn’t have to go to bed so

34
00:03:26.669 --> 00:03:32.737
early. That’s why I took off my shoes so I could scare you, no, not you, but . .

35
00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:39.298
." The man struggled for words in a very strong foreign accent. "Anyway, I’m very sorry."

36
00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:44.002
Then he gave Jo the warmest and friendliest smile she had ever seen.

37
00:03:44.003 --> 00:03:50.952
By now Jo had caught her breath and was doing her best not to stare at his bare toe, which was wiggling as he spoke.

38
00:03:50.953 --> 00:03:54.647
"I’m Friedrich Bauer," the man introduced himself.

39
00:03:54.648 --> 00:04:02.742
"Oh, you’re the Professor," said Jo, recognizing the name. "I’m Jo March, the girls’ new governess."

40
00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:07.235
"What a pleasure it is to finally make your acquaintance," he said kindly.

41
00:04:07.236 --> 00:04:15.539
The Professor, according to Mrs. Kirke, was originally from Germany but had come to America a few years ago along with his two orphaned nephews.

42
00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:23.822
He used to teach at a famous university in Berlin, but now he made his living as a tutor while caring for his deceased sister’s two boys.

43
00:04:23.823 --> 00:04:31.683
Mrs. Kirke said the Professor came from a wealthy and powerful family in Germany, but in his new country, he was almost penniless.

44
00:04:31.684 --> 00:04:36.208
"Yes, the girls speak of you all the time," the Professor was saying.

45
00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:40.643
"They even showed me what you have taught them. For example . . ."

46
00:04:40.644 --> 00:04:49.347
Then to Jo’s horror, the Professor began whistling a merry tune she had taught the girls.

47
00:04:49.348 --> 00:04:56.846
Blushing with shame, Jo, who had warned the girls sternly not ever to whistle in public because it was not ladylike, stuttered,

48
00:04:56.847 --> 00:05:01.923
"Well, I . . . the girls . . . just having fun."

49
00:05:01.924 --> 00:05:09.604
With a twinkle in his eye, he continued, "So I taught them a melody I am particularly fond of. It goes like this."

50
00:05:09.605 --> 00:05:13.752
The Professor began whistling another cheery tune.

51
00:05:13.753 --> 00:05:19.958
This time, Jo could not help laughing as the Professor did a little dance to go with his song.

52
00:05:19.959 --> 00:05:26.709
When his performance ended with a little bow, Jo clapped politely.

53
00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:31.737
"May I ask what you are reading?" he asked, peering at the book on her lap.

54
00:05:31.738 --> 00:05:39.340
Jo held it up and said, "I know I should be reading something more educational, but I just love mysteries and adventures."

55
00:05:39.341 --> 00:05:43.766
Shrugging, the Professor said, "No, you should read what you enjoy.

56
00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:48.350
You are the director of your life, not someone else.

57
00:05:48.351 --> 00:05:54.007
Live in a way that will bring you happiness, not in the way others expect you to live it."

58
00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:58.600
Appreciating his words, Jo asked the Professor to join her for a cup of tea.

59
00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:04.155
"Thank you very much," he said as he sat down in the chair next to Jo.

60
00:06:04.156 --> 00:06:09.118
When he crossed his left leg and saw the hole in his sock, he quickly switched to the other leg.

61
00:06:09.119 --> 00:06:16.820
Then he let out a loud, hearty laugh. "Rest assured, Miss March," he said.

62
00:06:16.821 --> 00:06:20.545
"It is not my habit to purposely show my toes to young ladies."

63
00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:24.381
Laughing along with him, Jo took a moment to study the Professor.

64
00:06:24.382 --> 00:06:30.911
He was older than she expected, perhaps in his mid-thirties, which, in comparison with her twenty years, was quite elderly.

65
00:06:30.912 --> 00:06:37.529
Yet he had a youthful energy. He also had an inviting warm smile and soft, expressive eyes.

66
00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:45.685
He was not handsome, like Laurie, but his face was interesting, and Jo found herself staring at it longer than she should have.

67
00:06:45.686 --> 00:06:49.940
"Well, Miss March . . . ," the Professor said in his strong German accent.

68
00:06:49.941 --> 00:06:57.099
"Please, call me Jo," she interrupted. "And by the way, if I may say so, your English is excellent, sir."

69
00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.544
"Thank you kindly, Jo," he said, pronouncing her name like "Cho."

70
00:07:01.545 --> 00:07:08.892
"Then you must call me Fritz, agreed? If you like, tomorrow I will prepare a special German hot chocolate drink.

71
00:07:08.893 --> 00:07:11.794
Shall we meet here in the library at the same time?"

72
00:07:11.795 --> 00:07:20.486
"I would love to, Fritz," Jo replied, not understanding why her heart suddenly began to beat faster.

